      "text": "The length of the parametric curve described by $(x,y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)$ from $t = 0$ to $t = \\pi$ is given by the integral of the square root of the sum of the squares of the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$. The general formula for the length of a parametric curve from $t = a$ to $t = b$ is given by:\n\\[ \\text{Length} = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt. \\]\nWe have $x = 2 \\sin t$ and $y = 2 \\cos t$. Therefore, $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t$. Then we proceed to solve:\n\\[ \\text{Length} = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt = 2t \\left|_{0}^{\\pi} = 2\\pi. \\]\nTherefore, the length of the curve is $\\boxed{2\\pi}$.",
