      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the length of a parametric curve of the form (x, y) = (f(t), g(t)), we use the arc length formula for the differential arc length (ds) as follows:\n\n\\[ds = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\\]\n\nApplying this formula to the given parametric equations:\n\n\\[(x, y) = (2\\sin t, 2\\cos t),\\]\n\nwe differentiate \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2\\cos t,\\]\n\\[\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2(-\\sin t) = -2\\sin t.\\]\n\nHence, the differential arc length (ds) is:\n\n\\[ds = \\sqrt{(2\\cos t)^2 + (-2\\sin t)^2} \\, dt.\\]\n\nWe simplify this expression:\n\n\\[ds = \\sqrt{4\\cos^2t + 4\\sin^2t} \\, dt,\\]\n\\[ds = \\sqrt{4(\\cos^2t + \\sin^2t)} \\, dt,\\]\n\\[ds = \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt,\\]\n\\[ds = \\sqrt{4} \\, dt,\\]\n\\[ds = 2 \\, dt.\\]\n\nNow, to find the length of the curve from \\(t=0\\) to \\(t=\\pi\\), we integrate the differential arc length:\n\n\\[L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt,\\]\n\\[L = \\left[2t\\right]_{0}^{\\pi},\\]\n\\[L = (2\\pi-0),\\]\n\\[L = 2\\pi.\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve \\((x, y) = (2\\sin t, 2\\cos t),\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}.\\)",
