      "text": "To find the length of a parametric curve given by \\((x,y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we use the arc length formula for parametric curves. The arc length formula is:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we compute the derivatives of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 2 \\sin t \\implies \\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 \\cos t \\implies \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFactor out the 4:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nRecall the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nNow, integrate the constant function 2 with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 1 \\, dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
