      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we can use the arc length formula. The arc length \\(L\\) of a parametric curve \\((x(t), y(t))\\) from \\(t = a\\) to \\(t = b\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to compute the derivatives \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\). Given:\n\\[\nx(t) = 2 \\sin t, \\quad y(t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\nThe derivatives are:\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t, \\quad \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the expression inside the square root:\n\\[\n(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\), we get:\n\\[\n4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the arc length integral simplifies to:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
